---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Hale playground">
  <section class="container prose">
    <span class="tag">/playground</span>
    <h1>Playground</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Run real Hale in your browser. Each example is compiled to
      <code>WebAssembly</code> with <code>hale build --target wasm32</code> and runs
      fully client-side — the runtime is the same compiler you install locally.
    </p>

    <div class="play-frame">
      <iframe src="/play/" title="Hale playground" loading="lazy"></iframe>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/play/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open full screen ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/getting-started/install">Install locally instead →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <strong>Next:</strong> write &amp; share <em>your own</em> code. Hale has no
      interpreter — <code>hale run</code> compiles through LLVM — so an arbitrary-code
      playground needs a small server that compiles your source to wasm and hands it
      back for the browser to run in its sandbox. That compile service is written in
      Hale itself (dogfooding) and validated end-to-end; hosting it is the remaining
      step.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .play-frame {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border, #2a2f3a);
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #0d1117;
  }
  .play-frame iframe {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 640px;
    border: 0;
  }
</style>
